cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
title: "PhylogenyPro: a browser-based platform for molecular phylogenetics, from the alignment to the dated tree"
abstract: >-
  PhylogenyPro is a self-contained web application that carries a set of sequences through a
  whole phylogenetic study in the browser, with no installation and no server-side computation.
  It reads FASTA, PHYLIP, NEXUS, Clustal, MEGA and GenBank files and morphological matrices,
  aligns them with a progressive aligner that can work on codons, and lets the alignment be
  trimmed and edited by hand. Each partition declares its genome — chloroplast, mitochondrion,
  nuclear ribosomal or low-copy nuclear — and that declaration is carried into every later step.
  It selects among 56 DNA models, twelve empirical amino-acid matrices and Mk/Mkv for
  morphology by AIC, AICc and BIC, and finds partition schemes by greedy merging; infers trees
  by parsimony, distance methods, maximum likelihood with NNI and SPR search, and
  Metropolis-coupled Bayesian MCMC, both of the last two running in Web Workers; measures
  support by bootstrap, ultrafast bootstrap, SH-aLRT, aBayes and posterior probabilities, and
  compares topologies with the KH, SH and AU tests. It then tests the molecular clock, dates the
  tree by least squares, penalised likelihood or a relaxed clock with fossil calibrations on the
  geological time scale; fits models of diversification; reconstructs continuous and discrete
  ancestral characters with stochastic mapping, BM/OU/EB, independent contrasts, PGLS,
  Blomberg's K and Pagel's lambda; reconstructs ancestral areas with DEC, DIVALIKE and
  BAYAREALIKE with and without founder-event speciation; and compares trees by five distances,
  concordance factors, quartets, split networks and ABBA-BABA. Throughout, an interpretation
  guide places each computed number on its own scale, and a calibration assistant turns a
  fossil, a geological event or a known rate into a prior and says what it means in years.
  It ends in a tree studio with pictures of the OTUs and clades and a cut into k groups, a
  figure studio that restyles every figure in the twelve blocks at once, figures at any
  resolution, a methods section written from what was actually run, and a reproducible package
  the program writes itself. Every block is checked against an independent implementation in R.
type: software
